1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,832
A woman admits that she once tried to be what men wanted her to be.

2
00:00:04,832 --> 00:00:06,278
Then she starts walking.

3
00:00:06,278 --> 00:00:12,825
At the end of the poem, the old order prepares a grand ceremony to honour her, and she simply does not turn up.

4
00:00:13,033 --> 00:00:14,493
Nothing is smashed.

5
00:00:14,493 --> 00:00:15,899
Nobody shouts.

6
00:00:15,899 --> 00:00:21,956
The rebellion is a walk and an empty chair, and that is what makes it frightening to the people it is aimed at.

7
00:00:27,433 --> 00:00:34,610
Welcome to Class 12 English, Unit 10: I Was My Own Route, by the Puerto Rican poet Julia de Burgos.

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:38,082
Six parts.

9
00:00:38,082 --> 00:00:39,502
Julia de Burgos.

10
00:00:39,502 --> 00:00:41,923
Reading a poem in translation.

11
00:00:41,923 --> 00:00:44,319
The walk, stanza by stanza.

12
00:00:44,319 --> 00:00:45,569
The empty chair.

13
00:00:45,569 --> 00:00:46,728
The themes.

14
00:00:46,728 --> 00:00:48,537
And the form and devices.

15
00:00:52,900 --> 00:01:04,347
Julia de Burgos was born in nineteen fourteen in Puerto Rico, the eldest of thirteen children in a very poor family; six of her brothers and sisters died of malnutrition.

16
00:01:04,347 --> 00:01:13,748
She became a teacher at twenty one, and a nationalist: for her, independence for the island, dignity for the poor and freedom for women were one cause.

17
00:01:13,748 --> 00:01:17,524
This poem comes from her first book, from nineteen thirty eight.

18
00:01:17,524 --> 00:01:24,373
She later lived in New York and Cuba, and died in poverty in Spanish Harlem in nineteen fifty three.

19
00:01:24,373 --> 00:01:29,027
Today she is a national figure, and a forerunner of Latina writers.

20
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:35,859
One thing most notes leave out.

21
00:01:35,859 --> 00:01:41,380
De Burgos wrote in Spanish; you read Jack Agüeros's English translation.

22
00:01:41,380 --> 00:01:47,356
Images, structure and argument survive translation, so discuss them with confidence.

23
00:01:47,356 --> 00:01:55,651
But sound and rhythm belong to the translator, so never say the poet uses alliteration without adding, in the English version.

24
00:01:55,651 --> 00:02:02,929
And something is lost: Spanish adjectives mark the speaker as a woman in every line; English cannot.

25
00:02:02,929 --> 00:02:05,507
The free verse shape, though, is hers.

26
00:02:05,507 --> 00:02:07,664
One sentence in the exam is enough.

27
00:02:12,433 --> 00:02:15,221
Stanza one opens with a confession.

28
00:02:15,221 --> 00:02:22,669
She wanted to be what men wanted her to be, and that was only an attempt at life: a rough draft, not a life.

29
00:02:22,669 --> 00:02:28,047
A game of hide and seek with her own being, in which she both hides and seeks.

30
00:02:28,047 --> 00:02:31,810
Then, only four lines in, but: I was made of nows.

31
00:02:31,810 --> 00:02:37,526
A coined plural: she is built of present moments, owing nothing to the past.

32
00:02:37,526 --> 00:02:46,807
Her feet refuse to walk backwards and go forward, forward, mocking the ashes of what is burnt out, towards the kiss of new paths.

33
00:02:48,033 --> 00:02:51,459
Stanza two, three short lines, is the cost.

34
00:02:51,459 --> 00:02:58,660
At every step the old guard, the defenders of the old order, beat their desperate wings and tear her back.

35
00:02:58,660 --> 00:03:02,540
Her back, because she has turned away and will not turn round.

36
00:03:02,540 --> 00:03:07,410
And desperate: they are not calm and powerful; they are frightened.

37
00:03:07,410 --> 00:03:14,219
Stanza three is release: she was unpinned forever, like a branch freed from a wall.

38
00:03:14,219 --> 00:03:23,087
And the paradox: every whiplash only drives her gaze further from the distant, familiar horizons she was taught to aim at.

39
00:03:23,087 --> 00:03:24,810
Punishment produces vision.

40
00:03:26,033 --> 00:03:31,686
Stanza four: having left her inherited ground, she claims all ground.

41
00:03:31,686 --> 00:03:45,930
She is a blossom of all the soils of the earth: soil without history, so no ancestors to obey; without a future, so no destiny mapped out; soil without edges, so no borders.

42
00:03:45,930 --> 00:03:51,256
Then stanza five is a single line, standing alone: the climax.

43
00:03:51,256 --> 00:03:53,860
All in me, as life was in me.

44
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:58,990
The hide and seek is over; the self and life are the same size.

45
00:03:58,990 --> 00:04:01,502
And the trailing dots refuse to close.

46
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:10,117
The last stanza repeats the first four lines word for word.

47
00:04:10,117 --> 00:04:13,098
It is not laziness; it is a test.

48
00:04:13,098 --> 00:04:16,002
Play the opening again, and see what has changed.

49
00:04:16,002 --> 00:04:24,557
Now the old guard hold a regal parade, and heralds announce her name: the establishment is opening its ranks to honour her.

50
00:04:24,557 --> 00:04:31,132
And at that very moment, the wish to follow men warped in her, twisted out of shape like damp wood.

51
00:04:31,132 --> 00:04:35,872
The final image is an absence: the homage was left waiting for me.

52
00:04:35,872 --> 00:04:38,463
No speech, no slammed door.

53
00:04:38,463 --> 00:04:39,666
She is simply elsewhere.

54
00:04:44,433 --> 00:04:45,398
Themes.

55
00:04:45,398 --> 00:04:50,203
Identity is found within, not handed out by anyone's approval.

56
00:04:50,203 --> 00:04:56,310
Women and patriarchy: a life shaped to what men want is only an attempt at life.

57
00:04:56,310 --> 00:05:01,362
The present over the past: made of nows, mocking the ashes.

58
00:05:01,362 --> 00:05:11,557
And quiet rebellion: no violence, just walking forward and declining an invitation, and the old guard's desperate fear measures her power.

59
00:05:11,557 --> 00:05:18,912
Remember too her politics: soil without edges, from a woman whose island was ruled by another country.

60
00:05:23,700 --> 00:05:31,983
The form is free verse, with no rhyme and no metre in either language, so the shape on the page is de Burgos's own.

61
00:05:31,983 --> 00:05:37,725
Six stanzas of nine, three, ten, six, one and eight lines.

62
00:05:37,725 --> 00:05:44,743
The short stanza two is the bruise; the single line of stanza five is the climax, standing alone.

63
00:05:44,743 --> 00:05:50,774
And the structure is circular: the last stanza repeats the first, and ends differently.

64
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:53,447
Devices to quote.

65
00:05:53,447 --> 00:05:57,418
Metaphor: an attempt at life, and made of nows.

66
00:05:57,418 --> 00:06:02,288
The old guard and their desperate wings as a symbol of frightened tradition.

67
00:06:02,288 --> 00:06:06,611
Paradox: each whiplash makes her see more clearly.

68
00:06:06,611 --> 00:06:11,611
Repetition: forward, forward, the beat of footsteps.

69
00:06:11,611 --> 00:06:17,274
And the long closing ellipsis: the homage still waiting, the future unwritten.

70
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:20,470
Pause and think.

71
00:06:27,767 --> 00:06:32,440
Her wish to follow men "warped"; the homage was left waiting for her.

72
00:06:34,433 --> 00:06:34,868
One more.

73
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:47,617
The English sound effects are the translator's; images and structure are de Burgos's.

74
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:50,895
Quick recap.

75
00:06:50,895 --> 00:06:54,697
De Burgos, and a poem read in translation.

76
00:06:54,697 --> 00:07:00,114
An attempt at life, a woman made of nows, an old guard with desperate wings.

77
00:07:00,114 --> 00:07:03,747
An unpinned branch and soils without edges.

78
00:07:03,747 --> 00:07:07,103
And a circular ending where the homage is left waiting.

79
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:12,864
That was I Was My Own Route.

80
00:07:12,864 --> 00:07:15,585
Next poem: The Awakening Age.

81
00:07:15,585 --> 00:07:16,169
See you there.

